  "HOW1": "Each filter uses the passbands and transmission published by the manufacturer (estimated data are flagged in the filter catalogue). In each band the object’s signal (continuum plus Hα, [NII], Hβ, OIII and [SII] lines) and the sky background are computed: the site’s SQM by direction (LED-like continuum plus mercury and sodium lines), natural airglow, the Moon in 5-minute steps, atmospheric extinction reduced with elevation. The collected signal depends on the sensor’s spectral response (efficiency measured on back-illuminated Sony sensors: 58% of peak at Hα, 54% at SII, 100% at OIII) and, on colour cameras, on the transmission of the red, green and blue pixel filters. The target is followed along its path on the chosen night. The time shown is the moonless time along that path; with the Moon of the night the time is recomputed and, if another combination is at least 15% faster, it is shown separately.",
  "HOW2": "The signal-to-noise ratio (SNR) is computed per resolution element, proportional to the aperture (2.3″ at 200 mm, 4.7″ at 100 mm, or the pixel if larger), on three structures: the object’s main body (for emission nebulae from the Hα measured in the NSNS and SHASSA surveys), the faint parts (haloes, outer arms) and the surrounding dust. Time follows photon noise: t ∝ SNR² × (signal + background + read noise) / signal². Filters, sky, telescope and Moon change the time only through this relation.",
  "HOW3": "The “good” level is the SNR reached by the object’s median well-rated AstroBin photo, computed with each photo’s equipment and sky (declared SQM, or the Bortle class converted with the values the authors themselves declare) and transferred to a colour camera under an SQM 19 sky. The transfer uses effects measured on the photos: for the same object, a mono camera reaches a 4 times higher SNR², and 1.7 times higher per magnitude of darker sky, because hours change little with the setup. For objects without photos the level is predicted from the object’s physical difficulty. Calibrated on 6,918 photos of 311 objects (colour, DSLR and mono cameras, skies from Bortle 1 to 9); validated on objects never used for calibration: typical error per object a factor of 1.6 for colour photos from cities, 2 for all photos. The same shot by different people already varies by a factor of 2.7 in hours.",
  "HOW4": "Filter choice: among the combinations allowed by the profile’s goal (shortest time to the SNR, all lines, natural colours) the one with the shortest time is chosen, with no extra weights. Limits: the model does not know optical quality, focus, actual transparency and processing, which can change the result by a factor of 2; the site’s SQM is often an estimate (±0.3 mag or more) and the plan shows the resulting range of hours. The times are for planning, not guarantees.",
